KINGDOMS
--------
Guild territory on the overhead map - the full rules

Syntax:   kingdom <subcommand>

THIS IS THE LONG ONE. Two help entries cover kingdoms, and they are not
the same text:

  help kingdom    the short entry - what a kingdom is, the subcommand
                  list, and the few rules that catch people out.
  help kingdoms   this file - the whole rulebook: the ring order, where
                  a hall may stand, the arrears ladder, dormancy,
                  guards, and the resource economy in full.

Read the short one to play. Read this one when you want to know exactly
what the game will do. THIS FILE IS AUTHORITATIVE: the short entry is a
summary of it and nothing else, so where the two ever differ, this text
is right and the short entry is stale.

A guild that has raised a guildhall may go further and become a KINGDOM:
a guild that holds land. Territory is measured in map squares, it is
claimed one square at a time, it costs coin to take and coin to keep, and
it can be lost again if the treasury runs dry.

Kingdoms are an optional system. If your server has not enabled them the
command will say so, and nothing below applies.


THE SHAPE OF A REALM
--------------------
A realm is not drawn freehand. It grows outward from the guildhall
entrance in four concentric rings:

        ring 1     3x3      8 squares
        ring 2     5x5     16 squares
        ring 3     7x7     24 squares
        ring 4     9x9     32 squares

That is 80 claimable squares, plus the hall's own square in the middle -
81 in all, a nine-by-nine block with the hall at its heart.

The order is fixed. Within a ring, land is taken CLOCKWISE beginning due
NORTH of the hall, and a ring must be finished before the next one opens.
You cannot skip ahead, reach for a corner, or take a square out of turn.
'kingdom claim' always takes the next square in the sequence, and
'kingdom map' shows you which one that is.

                77 78 79 80 49 50 51 52 53
                76 46 47 48 25 26 27 28 54
                75 45 23 24  9 10 11 29 55
                74 44 22  8  1  2 12 30 56
                73 43 21  7  @  3 13 31 57
                72 42 20  6  5  4 14 32 58
                71 41 19 18 17 16 15 33 59
                70 40 39 38 37 36 35 34 60
                69 68 67 66 65 64 63 62 61

The @ is your hall. The numbers are the order the squares are taken in.


WHERE A REALM MAY STAND
-----------------------
Every one of the 80 squares must be legal ground BEFORE THE GUILDHALL IS
BUILT - not later, when you get round to claiming it. A hall may only be
raised where a complete realm could one day fit, so a site that suits the
hall itself can still be refused because square sixty-one falls in open
water or off the edge of the map.

The rules, each checked against every square:

  - There must be a room there. Much of the world map is empty space
    with nothing behind it, and empty space cannot be claimed.
  - The ground must be settleable. Open water and impassable terrain
    are out.
  - THE UNDERDARK IS CLOSED TO REALMS ENTIRELY, for every race and
    every alignment. There are no underground kingdoms.
  - Not too close to a hometown, so a realm cannot wall a city in.
  - Not too close to a zone entrance, so a realm cannot bottle up the
    way into an area.
  - Clear of every other realm. Two realms may not even touch: there is
    always at least one unclaimed square between them.
  - No guildhall may stand on it - not another guild's, and not one of
    your own outposts either. A hall has its own square; a realm
    cannot take it.

FIND GROUND BEFORE YOU BUILD ON IT. 'kingdom prospect' judges the
square you are standing on as a seat, and when it will not serve it
says which square of the eighty is refused, why, which way the trouble
lies and how far off it is - then names the nearest squares that WOULD
take a realm, by distance and compass point. It needs no guild and no
realm, because it is the verb for deciding whether founding one is
worth it at all.

'kingdom map' judges the ground square by square and lists exactly what
would refuse you. A guild with no realm yet is shown the ground its own
guildhall stands on, so a site can be read before it is committed to.

Seats are scarce but not rare: a realm must keep five squares clear of
any hometown and five of any way into another zone, which leaves a few
thousand legal seats on the surface map. Walk, prospect, walk again.


TAKING AND LOSING LAND
----------------------
Claiming costs COIN AND WORKED MATERIAL both. The coin comes from the
guild treasury; the material comes from the realm's own stores - what
your people have banked with 'kingdom harvest'.

Each square costs more than the one before it, COMPOUNDING, so the
outer ring is a different order of undertaking from the first. On the
shipped settings the first square is 1,000 platinum, and the corners
of the curve run:

    square      coin        running total
         1     1,000 p          1,000 p
         8     1,407 p          9,549 p   (ring 1 complete)
        24     3,071 p         44,501 p   (ring 2 complete)
        48     9,905 p        188,024 p   (ring 3 complete)
        80    47,201 p        971,221 p   (the whole map)

ALL FOUR RESOURCES, EVERY SQUARE. Mineral, wood, fibre and water are
each wanted, and a realm short of even one of them pays nothing and
claims nothing - so no realm expands on one sort of ground alone. The
material rides the same curve as the coin: 25 of each for the first
square, about 1,180 of each for the eightieth.

The inner rings are within reach of a guild of ten. The fourth is
meant to be the work of a guild that means it.

Holding land costs coin as well. Upkeep is charged on every square you
own, once per REAL WEEK - it is a standing obligation on the guild
rather than a tax on being logged in. If the treasury cannot pay, the
realm decays in this
order:

  1. The guards go home.
  2. The realm stops banking what its people gather.
  3. One OUTER RING is lost per further missed payment.

Depositing coin does not itself lift a rung. The ladder unwinds at the
next reckoning, when the charge is actually met: that payment stops the
slide wherever it has reached, and in the same cycle the guards march
back and the stores begin filling again. LAND ALREADY LOST DOES NOT
RETURN ON PAYMENT - a ring that has reverted must be claimed again from
scratch, at full price.

A REALM IN ARREARS MAY NOT CLAIM. While upkeep is owed 'kingdom claim'
is refused outright, so a guild cannot buy back the very ring it is
losing; settle the debt and the next payment reopens it. A realm that
the ladder has stripped all the way back to the hall square owes
nothing, and with nothing owed there is nothing to settle: it may
begin claiming again from square one at once. That first square steps
it off the ladder, so its guards and its stores come back with the
land.

'kingdom abandon' gives up your most recent square voluntarily. It
asks first and takes nothing until you type 'kingdom abandon confirm',
because there is no refund. Territory always stays contiguous, so it
is always the last square taken that goes first. Abandoning is allowed
even in arrears: shedding squares is how a realm shrinks its upkeep
back to something it can pay.

Converting, claiming and abandoning are the LEADER'S acts alone. They
spend the treasury and change what the guild owes every cycle, and no
lesser rank may do them.


THE HALL AND THE REALM
----------------------
A realm is territory only because a guildhall stands at its centre,
and it lives or dies with the hall.

If the main hall MOVES, the claims follow it: the same count of
squares is laid out again around the new site, in the same order. The
new ground is judged first, every square of it, exactly as at
placement. If any square is refused the claims do not move and the
realm goes DORMANT instead.

If the main hall is DESTROYED, or the guild is left with outposts
only, the realm goes dormant likewise. DORMANCY STICKS: a realm is
anchored only while a MAIN guildhall of its guild actually stands on
the square the realm records, so the room the hall stood in being
still there wakes nothing. Nothing wakes a realm but a hall.

A dormant realm is not dissolved. Its claims are kept, and so is the
record of where its hall stood - but it holds no ground. Not one
square answers to it, so for every question the world asks about
ownership its land is simply unclaimed. Its guards go home the moment
the hall does, and none may be posted while it sleeps. 'kingdom
status', 'kingdom map' and 'kingdom guards' will not report on it at
all: each answers only that the guildhall cannot be found, so the
realm has no centre. Nothing can be claimed, and nothing abandoned.

NO UPKEEP IS CHARGED while it sleeps, and the steward says so once a
cycle, whatever the realm holds on paper. A debt already on the
ladder is neither advanced nor forgiven: the rung it slept on is the
rung it wakes on. Nor does the sleep run up a bill - the first
reckoning after it wakes asks one cycle's upkeep, not one for every
cycle it slept.

Its stores still take what its people gather, and the treasury still
takes coin, so a guild can pay down its debt and lay in resources
while it rebuilds. What lapses with the hall is the land's advantage:
with no centre to measure from, none of the realm's squares count as
favouring anything, and its gatherers draw as though the realm held
nothing at all.

Raise a main hall again and the realm wakes with its claims intact -
on the old square at once, or on a new one only if all eighty squares
of the new footprint pass the same judging as a move. If one square is
refused it stays dormant.

If the GUILD ITSELF IS DELETED, the realm is deleted with it: the land
is released, the guards are dismissed and the stores are gone. There
is nothing to inherit; a guild founded afterwards starts from nothing.


GUARDS
------
GUARDS ARE BOUGHT, NOT CONJURED BY OWNING LAND. Land sets the ceiling -
one guard for every few squares held - and the guild pays for each one
it raises, names its calling, and promotes it as the realm grows. A
realm that claims its way to eighty squares and buys nothing fields
nothing.

  kingdom roster              every guard: number, class, level, and
                              what the next promotion costs.
  kingdom hire <class>        raise a new one. (leader)
  kingdom promote <#> [class] raise one to the next realm tier, and
                              re-school it if you name a class. (leader)

A new guard costs 5,000 platinum and stands at level 45. HOW HIGH IT
MAY RISE IS SET BY YOUR COMPLETED RINGS. The first ring unlocks level
50; each later ring adds two levels:

    ring 1 complete   (8 squares)    level 50
    ring 2 complete   (24 squares)   level 52
    ring 3 complete   (48 squares)   level 54
    ring 4 complete   (80 squares)   level 56

Each promotion costs a quarter of the span up to 20,000 platinum, so a
guard taken all the way to 56 has cost exactly that in total. PROMOTION
IS ONE-WAY. A guard's level never falls - not on arrears, not on land
lost, not on request - and there is no refund and no demote verb.

Losing land lowers the ceiling for the NEXT promotion and takes no
level already paid for. It can put a guard on the bench: the roster
keeps everyone bought, and only as many as the land permits take the
field, the rest returning when the ground does.

THE GARRISON PATROLS. Guards walk the realm's own squares and will not
step off them, so they cannot be lured across a border by someone
standing outside it. They see invisible, sense life, see in the dark
and by ultravision, fly and swim, cannot be summoned, charmed,
paralysed or frightened, and stand under a globe of invulnerability and
stoneskin. They are aggressive to the opposing racewar side. Strike one
and it calls the garrison: it shouts, and every other guard of that
realm that is free converges on the noise.

They stand on your land. They do not follow you and they are not a
bodyguard. The count is a ceiling, not a promise: a realm with upkeep
unpaid may field none at all, the guards being the first rung of the
ladder, and a dormant realm may field none either, having no ground to
post them on.


THE CHAMPION
------------
A realm holding ALL EIGHTY SQUARES - and only such a realm - may raise
one champion, for 25,000 platinum:

  kingdom champion <class> <class>     (leader)

It is the module's only multiclass mob: name two callings and they must
differ. It stands at level 60, keeps everything a guard keeps, and adds
inertial barrier, blur, flurry, deflection and Stornog's greater
spheres. There is exactly one, it is never promoted, and losing a
square sends it home rather than unmaking it - it takes the field again
when the eightieth square is retaken, at no further cost.

THE BANNER IS WHAT THE CHAMPION IS FOR. It plants one where it stands:

  the banner of combat <realm>     a damage buff on every guard of the
                                   realm, wherever they are standing.
  the banner of sanctity <realm>   a heal on all of them, every tick.

It plants sanctity when the garrison is hurt and combat otherwise, and
will not raise a second while the first still stands.

A BANNER CAN BE DESTROYED, and that is the point of making it a thing
in a room rather than an aura. It is an object standing in the dirt,
it has hit points, and 'kill banner' tears at it. Break it and the
buff goes with it at once; the champion raises another five rounds
later. Whoever is attacking a realm therefore has a real choice: spend
those rounds on the banner, or fight a garrison that is being mended
every tick.


RESOURCES
---------
Harvest nodes are scattered at random across the whole world, the
surface and the Underdark alike. They are not kingdom property, and
they are not put on kingdom ground: a node is never placed on a square
a realm already holds, so the work is always found out in the world.
Claim the ground out from under one and it does not become yours: the
node is swept away, to load again somewhere else.

THEY ARE SCARCE ON PURPOSE. Only about eighty stand across the whole
surface map at any moment, and sixty through the Underdark - and that
is eighty of ALL kinds together, not eighty of each, so any one
resource has something like thirty-five nodes in the entire world at
once, shared by everybody. What kind any one of
them is was rolled when it loaded, so the mix shifts constantly and no
region is ever certain to be holding a spring or a seam of ore. Work
one out and it is gone from the world; before long another loads
somewhere else entirely, as likely as not a different kind in a room
nowhere near it. Finding one is meant to be worth something.

ANYONE MAY WORK A NODE. You need not belong to a kingdom, and you need
not be standing on anyone's land - if you find one, you can work it.
The work takes a few moments of steady effort and it tires you, and you
must be on your feet and out of a fight to begin. What you draw goes
into YOUR OWN realm's stores, so a gatherer with no realm behind them
fills nothing. The ground is worked all the same: the draw is spent
whoever swings, and what a landless gatherer cuts loose is left where
it lies.

A KINGDOM'S ADVANTAGE IS THE YIELD, NOT THE NODE. A realm draws best
on the resources its own ground produces - but only once it holds
ENOUGH of the right land. Every TWENTY owned squares that favour a
resource add one step to what your people draw of it, anywhere in the
world, so a young realm's first ring promises nothing yet. A draw is
worth its steps - one for the node itself, one for each grade of
richness above poor, one for each twenty favouring squares - and then
up to as many again on top, at random. So a realm with no forest to
its name takes one or two timber from a poor stand, and a full realm
of eighty forest squares takes five to ten from the same stand: FIVE
TIMES AS MUCH. The land's steps are added to the node's, not
multiplied by them, so the advantage is widest at a poor node and
narrows at a rich one - at a mother lode the same full realm takes
eight to sixteen against four to eight, twice as much. The comparison
is always between realms: a gatherer with no realm at all banks
nothing anywhere, however rich the ground. Ground counts by what it
is and by what it borders - a realm along the coast draws water better
though it can hold no water square - and a square that favours two
things counts for both. Territory buys a better hand at every node in
the world, not a fence around any of them.

Some ground is richer than others, and a node holds only so many draws;
take the last of them and it is worked out and gone. A node that has
been broken into does not wait for ever either: once the first draw is
taken it rots away after a time, whether or not anyone comes back to
finish it. What is lost is replaced: each region of the world is
restocked toward its accustomed TOTAL of nodes, all kinds counted
together, however hard it is worked - and every replacement rolls its
own kind, so the supply moves about rather than running down.

Nodes are drawn on the overhead map as a coloured letter - s for a
seam of stone, t for timber, f for fibre, w for water, and m for the
fungal stands of the Underdark. Everyone sees them, with no skill or
distance test to pass - even the deep forest that hides men and beasts
on the map does not hide a node, for a stand of timber IS the visible
feature of a forest - though anything else standing on the square is
drawn ahead of the node. Underdark nodes are drawn dimmer than surface
ones, and what grows below is not what grows above: an ore seam
for a seam of stone, a fungal stand for timber, cave silk for flax and
reed, a dark pool for a spring. They give up the same four things -
mineral, wood, fibre and water.

'kingdom survey' reports the node on the square you are standing on:
what it yields, how good the ground is, and how many draws are left.

BE CLEAR ABOUT THIS: RESOURCES PUT INTO A REALM CANNOT BE TAKEN OUT
AGAIN. They are not the guild bank and they are not spending money. They
exist to be spent on the realm itself, and once given they are given.

A realm TWO rungs into arrears banks nothing. One missed cycle costs
only the guards; the second stops the stores, and they stay stopped
until a reckoning the treasury can actually meet - paying coin in
between reckonings does not restart them by itself.


SUBCOMMANDS
-----------
kingdom            - with no argument, your realm's status, or the
                     short command summary while there is no realm.
kingdom status     - territory, rings, treasury standing, guards, stores.
kingdom map        - the nine-by-nine grid: what you hold, what is next,
                     and what is blocked. Before there is a realm it
                     judges the ground your guildhall stands on.
kingdom survey     - what the node on this square yields, how good the
                     ground is, and how many draws are left in it.
kingdom convert    - turn your guild into a kingdom. This grants NO land,
                     only the right to start claiming it. (leader)
kingdom claim      - take the next square in the sequence. (leader)
kingdom abandon    - give up your most recent square. Does nothing
                     until you add 'confirm', because nothing is
                     refunded. (leader)
kingdom harvest    - work the node on the square you are standing on.
kingdom deposit    - pay coin into the guild treasury that upkeep draws
                     on. Wants a bank and a coin string, like
                     '500 gold 20 silver'. Any member may pay in.
kingdom guards     - how large a garrison the realm may field.
kingdom roster     - every guard bought: number, class, level, and what
                     the next promotion would cost.
kingdom hire       - raise a new guard. Takes a class. (leader)
kingdom promote    - raise one guard to the next realm tier, naming it
                     by its number on the roster; add a class to
                     re-school it at the same time. (leader)
kingdom champion   - raise the realm's one champion. Takes TWO differing
                     classes, and wants all eighty squares. (leader)
kingdom prospect   - would a realm stand where you are? If not, why not,
                     and which way the nearest ground that would take
                     one lies. NEEDS NO GUILD - it is the verb for
                     deciding whether to found one at all.
kingdom help       - a short built-in summary, with your server's own
                     prices filled in. 'help kingdom' is the short help
                     entry; 'help kingdoms' is this text.

'kingdom c', 'kingdom s', 'kingdom h' and 'kingdom p' are ambiguous - c
is shared by claim and convert, s by status and survey, h by help,
harvest and hire, p by prospect and promote - and each will ask which
you meant rather than guess; claim and convert in particular both spend
the treasury's money.


WHO MAY USE IT, AND WHEN
------------------------
The command itself asks only that you are awake. You may read the
status, the map or the garrison while resting or lying down. Working
a node is the exception: to begin you must be standing and out of a
fight, and you must stay standing, awake and unengaged for the work to
finish, or it stops where it is.

'kingdom harvest' and 'kingdom survey' need no guild at all. Every
other verb is for FULL MEMBERS of a guild, and belonging to one is not
the same as being a full member of it: an applicant, a banned
character or one on parole has no realm to speak for, and nothing they
gather is banked either. Converting, claiming and abandoning are the
leader's alone, as above.

